  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 223 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Another cool day is expected as a seasonably chilly air \nmass remains overhead. Temperatures will linger in the 40s for most, \nwith a few 50s in the Upper Valley. The air mass is also dry, and \nmountain summits will feel especially crisp with dewpoints in the \nsingle digits in Fahrenheit heading below 0. Closer to the ground, \nanother day with relative humidity values ranging in the 30s, \nlocally down into the upper 20 percent range, is expected. \nFortunately, winds stay relatively light in the afternoon, but a few \ngusts to 15 mph are possible across Vermont. Dry air will result in \na chilly night in the teens to mid 20s tonight. The only thing \npreventing prime radiational cooling will be some incoming high \nclouds in northern New York. Nevertheless, a few climate sites will \nbe in striking distance of record lows.\n\nHigh pressure will amble offshore Wednesday, and more seasonable \ntemperatures are expected. A passing mid-level trough will slide \neast, and with it will be some increasing clouds. Low-level \nconditions will be much too dry for any precipitation to approach \nthe surface. Surface relative humidity values will be about the same \nwith light winds switching to the south midday.\n\nForecast weather for Thursday will be warmer with high temperatures \nclimbing into the 60s, but that will come with the price of \nincreasing south to southwesterly gusts west of the Greens into \nnorthern New York of 20-30 mph. This will signal an incoming cold \nfront, approaching the St. Lawrence Valley by Thursday evening. The \ntongue of moisture along the front is fairly narrow, and the \nsynoptic forcing is not anything noteworthy, but they should be \nenough to produce light rain. It will be in the process of decay, \nthough. So it is likely to produce more precipitation across \nnorthern areas than in south-central Vermont. Mainly a few \nhundredths up to a tenth is expected, mainly along northwest slopes \nof the northern Greens/northern Adirondacks and along the \ninternational border. A few showers will linger across northern \nmountains Friday afternoon, but trending dry gain into the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: In the wake of a frontal boundary moving through the \nregion late this week, high pressure looks to build over the region \nfor the weekend, bringing another period of dry and seasonable \nweather. High temperatures will generally climb into the 50s and low \n60s this weekend. Heading into the start of next week, there looks \nto be increasing chance for showers and an end to our dry weather \npattern, although there is still a lot of uncertainty amongst models \nand the overall pattern evolution at this time.",
